{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NRZrsEZsLBgreh3rCC8gkE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Checklist for `true-bdd build tests`.\n2\t#\n3\t# Walk every scenario in the registry and confirm it is executable: that\n4\t# every one of its Given/When/Then steps resolves to a step definition in\n5\t# the suite that owns it. The fix loop asks Claude to author the missing\n6\t# definition.\n7\t#\n8\t# The older contract — \"some test file mentions the scenario id\" — died\n9\t# with the feature file. A scenario is no longer copied into a spec that\n10\t# names it; it IS the spec, read at run time by the suite's runner, and\n11\t# what can be missing is the code its steps bind to. Grepping for the id\n12\t# would now pass on the registry itself.\n13\t\n14\tversion: \"2.0\"\n15\tlast_updated: \"2026-08-16\"\n16\t\n17\t# Model tier per AI role, from `engine.models` in true-bdd.yaml (never a\n18\t# model id). Omit a role to inherit its engine-level default; a prompt can\n19\t# override with `model:` / `fix_model:` / `apply_model:`.\n20\tengine:\n21\t  # Writing the missing step definition is a writing job, not a\n22\t  # reasoning one.\n23\t  apply_model: coder\n24\t\n25\tsections:\n26\t  - id: implementation\n27\t    name: \"Step Coverage\"\n28\t    description: \"Every step of every registry scenario must bind to a step definition in the suite that owns the scenario.\"\n29\t    validation_prompts:\n30\t      - Q: |\n31\t          Does every step of this scenario resolve to a step definition\n32\t          in the suite that owns it?\n33\t\n34\t          First find the suite. Read the architectural spec listed under\n35\t          Reference Documentation and look through\n36\t          the tree `tests/<service>/`, where `service:`\n37\t          equals this scenario's **Service**. That entry's `path:` is\n38\t          the suite's root; its step definitions live under\n39\t          `<path>/steps/`.\n40\t\n41\t          Then bind each step. A step definition is a call of the form\n42\t          `suite.Step(`<regexp>`, <func>)`. A step RESOLVES when exactly\n43\t          one registered regexp matches its text — the text alone, with\n44\t          the Given/When/Then/And keyword stripped, because a definition\n45\t          is bound by wording and not by which block a step sits in.\n46\t\n47\t          One exception, and it is not a loophole: a step whose text\n48\t          opens with `llm:` or `judge:` is run by a MODEL rather than by\n49\t          a step definition. It always resolves and it must never be\n50\t          given one — a definition matching `judge: …` would bind a\n51\t          clause written precisely because no pattern could settle it.\n52\t          Mark such a step MODEL-RUN and move on.\n53\t\n54\t          Pass when every step of the scenario resolves.\n55\t          Fail when any step resolves to none, or to more than one.\n56\t\n57\t          Context: emit one line per step, then a verdict.\n58\t            - \"step: <step text> -> <matching pattern, MODEL-RUN, or UNDEFINED>\"\n59\t            - \"Verdict: executable / <N> step(s) unbound\"\n60\t        rationale: \"A scenario no step definition binds is behaviour written down and never run; the registry would claim coverage the suite does not have.\"\n61\t        docs:\n62\t          - architecture_yaml\n63\t        F: |\n64\t          Author the missing step definition(s) in the suite that owns\n65\t          this scenario.\n66\t\n67\t          Steps:\n68\t            1. Read the architectural spec and find the\n69\t               tree `tests/<service>/`, where `service:`\n70\t               matches this scenario's. Its `path:` is the suite root.\n71\t            2. Read the existing definitions under `<suite path>/steps/`\n72\t               to learn the state type they share and the helpers\n73\t               already available. REUSE them — a new definition that\n74\t               re-implements what a sibling already does is the drift\n75\t               this command exists to prevent.\n76\t            3. For each unbound step, register one definition:\n77\t               `suite.Step(`^<anchored regexp>$`, <func>)`. Put a\n78\t               capture group where the step's text varies — a quoted\n79\t               run, a number — so one definition serves every scenario\n80\t               phrasing it that way, and do not write a definition whose\n81\t               pattern is a literal copy of one scenario's line.\n82\t            4. Implement the function against the suite's state type.\n83\t               Given prepares, When acts, Then asserts and returns an\n84\t               error naming what it expected and what it got.\n85\t            5. Do not weaken an existing definition to make this step\n86\t               match. Do not edit the registry.\n87\t            6. Never write a definition for a step whose text opens with\n88\t               `llm:` or `judge:`. Those are run by a model by the\n89\t               scenario author's explicit choice, and a pattern that\n90\t               captured one would take that choice back.\n91\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c8401fb-1a47-40af-8dc2-7e2f3cf0050e","uuid":"651e4d98-e841-4c30-b211-064d832bb5d0","timestamp":"2026-09-03T21:10:54.406Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"{{CWD}}/true-bdd/checklists/build-tests.yaml","content":"# Checklist for `true-bdd build tests`.\n#\n# Walk every scenario in the registry and confirm it is executable: that\n# every one of its Given/When/Then steps resolves to a step definition in\n# the suite that owns it. The fix loop asks Claude to author the missing\n# definition.\n#\n# The older contract — \"some test file mentions the scenario id\" — died\n# with the feature file. A scenario is no longer copied into a spec that\n# names it; it IS the spec, read at run time by the suite's runner, and\n# what can be missing is the code its steps bind to. Grepping for th